---
import BlogPost from '$lib/layouts/BlogPost.astro';
import Provider from '$lib/openrouter/Provider.astro';
import ProviderDialogs from '$lib/openrouter/ProviderDialogs.astro';
import UsageChart from '$lib/openrouter/UsageChart.astro';
import ClickableQuote from '$lib/ClickableQuote.astro';

export const title = 'GMI Cloud is a wrapper';
export const date = '2026-11-25';
---

<BlogPost {title} {date}>
  <p>
    OpenRouter <a
      href="https://web.archive.org/web/20261121184629/https://openrouter.ai/provider/gmicloud"
      >disabled all GMI Cloud endpoints</a
    > on November 20, after GMI Cloud told it an attacker had searched a system holding about three days
    of request content "for credentials in prompts". A security researcher's planted AWS keys, sent to
    GMI Cloud on October 21, 22, 27 and 28, were later used to try to create new users and keys. <a
      href="https://www.gmicloud.ai/en/blog/security-incident-affecting-gmi-cloud-endpoints-on-openrouter"
      >GMI Cloud's own notice</a
    > puts the exposure window at October 18 to November 18, and says it has now stopped logging prompts
    and responses.
  </p>
  <UsageChart of="gmicloud" title="GMI Cloud's daily tokens on OpenRouter" />
  <p>
    But the breach isn't the only thing GMI Cloud hasn't been upfront about. It markets itself as
    hosting models on its own infrastructure, yet in its TOS it self-identifies as a pure router:
  </p>
  <ClickableQuote
    href="https://web.archive.org/web/20261121185243/https://www.gmicloud.ai/en/legal/console-terms-of-service"
  >
    <p>
      Company operates a <mark>large language model aggregator</mark> where users may use its website
      [...] to <mark>access third-party application programming interfaces</mark> ("APIs") to use a variety
      of generative artificial intelligence models (section 1.1)
    </p>
  </ClickableQuote>
  <ClickableQuote
    href="https://web.archive.org/web/20261121185243/https://www.gmicloud.ai/en/legal/console-terms-of-service"
  >
    <p>
      The parties acknowledge and agree that Company <mark
        >acts solely as a technical intermediary</mark
      > that enables Client's access to third-party APIs and AI Models via the Site and provides billing,
      routing and account management functions in connection with such access, and: (a) <mark
        >does not provide or operate any APIs, AI Models</mark
      >, or other Vendor Products accessible during the Service (section 1.3)
    </p>
  </ClickableQuote>
  <p>
    There are hints of choice and sovereignty elsewhere, but they're dead ends. Like this quote from
    the MaaS Data Processing Addendum:
  </p>
  <ClickableQuote
    href="https://trust.gmicloud.ai/resources?s=khd9h595ulrfn3hbydlmx8&name=gmi-cloud-maa-s-data-processing-addendum"
  >
    <p>
      GMI makes no representation regarding Third Party Model Provider's retention, location, or
      data-use practices; <mark>Customer is responsible for selecting routes</mark> appropriate to its
      requirements. (section 3.1)
    </p>
  </ClickableQuote>
  <p>
    You can't select routes - as of writing, the console doesn't show or let you pick a route for
    any model. So when you send a request to GMI Cloud, you can't tell which company runs the model,
    and in GMI Cloud's case, the copy kept for troubleshooting wasn't safe either.
  </p>
  <p>Or this copy on their MaaS landing page:</p>
  <ClickableQuote href="https://archive.is/VDhLU">
    <p>
      <mark>Not Just An API Router.</mark> [...] When the models are fully hosted and operated by GMI,
      AI builders can focus on their core value proposition and products. [...] <mark
        >GMI hosts and operates critical models on its own datacenter infrastructure</mark
      >, ensuring consistent performance that routing-only platforms cannot guarantee.
    </p>
  </ClickableQuote>
  <p>This seems like puffery, since their TOS says otherwise.</p>
  <p>
    You can't get clarification either - despite emailing <code>support@gmicloud.ai</code>, <code
      >sales@gmicloud.ai</code
    >, <code>louisa.g@gmicloud.ai</code>, and going to their Discord, GMI Cloud has been completely
    unresponsive.
  </p>

  <h2>Disclosures</h2>
  <p>
    I got credits to survey providers from OpenRouter, which, as you read about, used to route to
    GMI Cloud. I work at Surplus Intelligence, an inference marketplace that routes requests to
    providers (most more trustworthy than GMI Cloud!). However, nobody paid for this post.
  </p>
  <ProviderDialogs />
</BlogPost>
<style>
  mark {
    background-color: var(--m3c-primary);
    color: var(--m3c-on-primary);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
</style>
